WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.940
The first film showed how Toll-like receptors free NF-κB within minutes.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.340
Once that powerful inflammatory alarm fires, why does it not kill the cell or wreck

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.800
the body? Follow what happens right on DNA.

4
00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:20.503
The answer starts right on that DNA.

5
00:00:22.163 --> 00:00:27.423
Along with inflammatory genes, emerald NF-κB turns on two brakes of its own.

6
00:00:28.643 --> 00:00:31.083
First, it makes fresh coral IκBα.

7
00:00:32.423 --> 00:00:37.463
That new inhibitor enters the nucleus, lifts the switch off DNA, and pulls it

8
00:00:37.463 --> 00:00:38.463
outside.

9
00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:42.037
Second, it turns on an enzyme called A20.

10
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:49.557
A20 strips the sky-blue lysine-63 scaffold chains upstream, shutting the relay off

11
00:00:49.557 --> 00:00:55.977
near its source. New IκBα takes time to make, so this negative feedback arrives

12
00:00:55.977 --> 00:01:02.997
late. Under a lasting stimulus, nuclear NF-κB therefore rises and falls in waves.

13
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:06.763
NF-κB also spreads the alarm.

14
00:01:08.023 --> 00:01:14.343
One target gene encodes tumor necrosis factor, or TNF, a secreted signaling protein

15
00:01:14.343 --> 00:01:15.483
called a cytokine.

16
00:01:17.063 --> 00:01:21.443
Amber TNF binds TNF receptor 1 on the same cell and its neighbors.

17
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:28.163
That single receptor sends two opposite messages at once: die, and stay alive.

18
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:31.103
Normally, stay alive wins.

19
00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:37.043
First, an immediate brake right at the receptor holds the death machinery back

20
00:01:37.043 --> 00:01:38.083
without new genes.

21
00:01:39.463 --> 00:01:43.143
Then NF-κB turns on survival genes that shield the cell.

22
00:01:44.503 --> 00:01:48.063
Block that survival arm and the same TNF kills the cell.

23
00:01:49.303 --> 00:01:52.243
That is why this is called the anti-death, death pathway.

24
00:01:53.317 --> 00:01:54.897
Now zoom out and predict.

25
00:01:56.597 --> 00:02:01.617
Normal mice collapse into shock from a heavy dose of purified bacterial endotoxin.

26
00:02:02.837 --> 00:02:06.817
Mice lacking working TLR4 shrug off that exact same dose.

27
00:02:08.137 --> 00:02:12.717
How do those endotoxin-resistant mice fare when they meet live Gram-negative

28
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:13.717
bacteria?

29
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:22.793
Far worse off, because they never raise the early alarm.

30
00:02:24.213 --> 00:02:28.033
Live bacteria multiply faster and kill them far more easily.

31
00:02:29.393 --> 00:02:30.873
Humans show both failures too.

32
00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:39.093
Children born without working MyD88 or IRAK4 suffer repeated severe infections with

33
00:02:39.093 --> 00:02:41.993
pus-forming bacteria, often with little fever.

34
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:48.733
At the other extreme, endotoxin circulating in some infections triggers widespread

35
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:51.993
TNF release, and septic shock can follow.

36
00:02:53.253 --> 00:02:58.233
Blood vessels widen and leak, blood pressure drops, and the host's own alarm injures

37
00:02:58.233 --> 00:03:03.733
organs. Human sepsis is more complex than endotoxin shock alone.

38
00:03:05.073 --> 00:03:09.953
Yet a modified endotoxin fragment, far less toxic, helps vaccines work.

39
00:03:11.017 --> 00:03:18.597
Scope matters. TNF receptor 1 also has an immediate brake on death, and separate

40
00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:22.097
cytosolic sensors detect viral nucleic acids.

41
00:03:23.337 --> 00:03:29.157
Within canonical Toll-like receptor signaling, NF-κB drives survival and brakes

42
00:03:29.157 --> 00:03:33.117
alongside inflammation, so defense usually spares the host.
